{"step_index":7,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T08:00:24Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-10T13:30:24+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-10T13:30:24+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/renu5/Downloads/college/College%20sem%205/ecm/ECM_COMPLETE_StudyGuide.md`\nTotal Lines: 658\nTotal Bytes: 46882\nShowing lines 1 to 658\nContent truncated: showing bytes 0-46080 of 46882. To see more, call this tool again with the same line range and ContentOffset=46080.\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: # Energy Conservation and Management (BE05019021)\r\n2: ## Complete Study Guide for Mid-Sem (30 Marks)\r\n3: ### Exam: 10/09/2026 | Chapters 1-3 | All Tier 1, Tier 2, and Key Tier 3 Topics\r\n4: \r\n5: ---\r\n6: \r\n7: # Chapter 1: Energy Scenario (CO-1)\n8: \n9: ## TOPIC 1: ENERGY CONSERVATION ACT 2001\n10: \n11: **Q: Discuss the salient features of Energy Conservation Act 2001. (7 marks)**\n12: \n13: The Energy Conservation Act was enacted in October 2001 in India. Its primary objective is to reduce the energy intensity of the Indian economy. The government has notified a list of designated consumers in 9 sectors, BEE star labeling, and ECBC norms under this act. \n14: \n15: Key features of the Act are:\n16: 1. The Central Government can specify energy consumption standards for appliances and equipment.\n17: 2. It mandates standards and labeling for equipment and appliances.\n18: 3. Designated consumers (large energy users) must appoint an energy manager, get energy audits done, and comply with norms.\n19: 4. It established the Bureau of Energy Efficiency (BEE) under the Ministry of Power.\n20: 5. It defines penalties for non-compliance with the rules.\n21: 6. It introduced the Energy Conservation Building Code (ECBC) for commercial buildings.\n22: 7. State Designated Agencies (SDAs) are established for implementation at the state level.\n23: 8. The Central Government can prohibit the manufacture or sale of equipment not meeting standards.\n24: 9. It sets energy consumption standards for notified equipment.\n25: 10. It requires the mandatory display of energy consumption labels on equipment.\n26: \n27: ## TOPIC 2: BEE SCHEMES\n28: \n29: **Q: Enlist the schemes of Bureau of Energy Efficiency (BEE) and discuss any two in detail. (7 marks)**\n30: \n31: The schemes of the Bureau of Energy Efficiency include:\n32: 1. Standards and Labeling Programme\n33: 2. Energy Conservation Building Code (ECBC)\n34: 3. Designated Consumers Programme\n35: 4. Demand Side Management (DSM)\n36: 5. Bachat Lamp Yojna\n37: 6. SME Energy Efficiency Programme\n38: 7. Agriculture DSM Programme\n39: 8. Municipal DSM Programme\n40: 9. National Mission on Enhanced Energy Efficiency (NMEEE)\n41: 10. Perform Achieve and Trade (PAT) Scheme\n42: \n43: Standards and Labeling Programme provides star ratings for appliances based on their energy consumption. It helps consumers make informed choices by showing the energy efficiency of a product. Products with higher star ratings consume less electricity and save money over time.\n44: \n45: Energy Conservation Building Code (ECBC) sets minimum energy performance standards for new commercial buildings. It applies to buildings with a connected load of 100 kW or more. It covers aspects like the building envelope, HVAC, and lighting to ensure the building consumes less energy compared to conventional designs.\n46: \n47: **Q: Enlist various schemes of BEE and its benefits. (3 marks)**\n48: \n49: The BEE schemes include the Standards and Labeling Programme, ECBC, PAT Scheme, Demand Side Management, and Bachat Lamp Yojna. These schemes help reduce energy consumption, lower electricity bills for consumers, and decrease the peak power demand on the grid. They also promote the use of energy-efficient appliances and sustainable building designs across the country.\n50: \n51: ## TOPIC 3: INDIAN ENERGY SCENARIO\n52: \n53: **Q: Write a short note on Indian Energy Scenario. (7 marks)**\n54: *(Note: For a 4-mark question \"Discuss sector-wise energy consumption scenario in India\", write only the first two paragraphs.)*\n55: \n56: India is the third largest energy consumer globally after China and the USA. The primary energy sources heavily rely on coal which meets about 55% of the demand. Oil accounts for about 30% and natural gas for about 8%. Hydro, nuclear, and renewables make up the rest. Sector-wise consumption shows Industry at the top with roughly 45% usage. Transport uses about 18%, Residential 23%, Agriculture 8%, and Commercial 6%. Coal dominates power generation and produces about 70% of the country's electricity.\n57: \n58: India faces a high import dependency. About 80% of crude oil and 50% of natural gas are imported. The growing population, rapid urbanization, and industrial growth continuously increase energy demand. \n59: \n60: Despite total high consumption, the per capita energy consumption is still low. India's per capita consumption is about 600 kgoe compared to the world average of 1800 kgoe. The government is heavily pushing for renewables to meet future demand. Initiatives like the National Solar Mission and a target of 500 GW of renewable energy capacity by 2030 are key steps in this direction.\n61: \n62: ## TOPIC 4: ENERGY SECURITY\n63: \n64: **Q: Define energy security. Enlist different strategies to achieve it. (3 marks)**\n65: \n66: Energy security means the continuous availability of energy in various forms, in sufficient quantities, and at reasonable prices. Strategies to achieve energy security include:\n67: 1. Diversifying energy sources.\n68: 2. Increasing domestic energy production.\n69: 3. Building strategic energy reserves.\n70: 4. Promoting renewable energy.\n71: 5. Implementing energy conservation measures.\n72: 6. Engaging in international energy cooperation.\n73: \n74: **Q: Define energy security. Enlist strategies and discuss the role of energy conservation in achieving energy security. (7 marks)**\n75: \n76: Energy security means the continuous availability of energy in various forms, in sufficient quantities, and at reasonable prices. Strategies to achieve it include diversifying energy sources, increasing domestic production, building strategic reserves, promoting renewables, implementing energy conservation, and international cooperation.\n77: \n78: Energy conservation plays a major role in achieving energy security. It directly reduces the total energy demand of the country. Lower demand means the country needs to import fewer fossil fuels like oil and gas. This reduces the financial burden and improves economic stability. Conservation also extends the life of existing domestic fossil fuel reserves. Using less energy lowers environmental impact and greenhouse gas emissions.\n79: \n80: ## TOPIC 5: CLASSIFICATION OF ENERGY\n81: \n82: **Q: Define: (i) Renewable Energy (ii) Non-Renewable Energy (iii) Per Capita Energy Consumption. (3 marks)**\n83: \n84: Renewable Energy comes from sources that are naturally replenished. Examples include solar, wind, hydro, biomass, and geothermal energy.\n85: Non-Renewable Energy comes from sources that get depleted and cannot be replenished in a human timescale. Examples include coal, oil, natural gas, and nuclear energy.\n86: Per Capita Energy Consumption is the total energy consumed by a country divided by its population. India's per capita consumption is about 600 kgoe per year.\n87: \n88: **Q: Differentiate between primary and secondary sources and give examples. (4 marks)**\n89: \n90: Primary sources are found directly in nature and used without conversion. Examples include coal, crude oil, natural gas, solar radiation, and wind.\n91: Secondary sources are derived from primary sources through a conversion process. Examples include electricity, petrol, diesel, LPG, and coke.\n92: \n93: ## TOPIC 6: ENERGY CONSERVATION AND ITS IM